{{C&M Subdivision}}
The '''Chicago and Milwaukee Subdivision''' (commonly referred to as the '''C&M Subdivision''' or '''C&M Sub''') is a [[railway]] line running between [[Chicago, Illinois]] and [[Milwaukee, Wisconsin]].  It is operated by [[Canadian Pacific Railway]] through its primary [[United States]] subsidiary, the [[Soo Line Railroad]].  This had previously been owned by the [[Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad]], commonly known as the Milwaukee Road.<ref name="milwaukee-rondout">{{cite web|url=http://donwinter.com/Railroad%20Infrastructure%20and%20Traffic%20Data/Trunk%20Routes/Milwaukee%20Road%20Hiawatha%20Routes/Route%20Descriptions/Milwaukee%20to%20Rondout.htm|title=Milwaukee to Rondout|author=Don Winter|accessdate=June 6, 2010}}</ref><ref name="a20-rondout">{{cite web|url=http://donwinter.com/Railroad%20Infrastructure%20and%20Traffic%20Data/Regions/Chicago/Route%20Descriptions/Rondout%20to%20Tower%20A20.htm|title=Rondout to Tower A20|author=Don Winter|accessdate=June 6, 2010}}</ref>  It carries [[Amtrak]]'s ''[[Empire Builder]]'' and ''[[Hiawatha Service]]'', as well as commuter trains operated on part of [[Metra]]'s [[Milwaukee District/North Line]] into Chicago.  The [[Union Pacific Railroad]] operates their [[Milwaukee Subdivision]], a former [[Chicago and North Western Railway|Chicago and North Western]] line, parallel to this route just to the east.

This line once carried the Milwaukee Road's fast ''[[Hiawatha (passenger train)|Hiawatha]]'' passenger trains, including trains that regularly exceeded {{convert|100|mph}}.  For several years in the 1940s and early 1950s, trains were scheduled to run along this stretch in 75 minutes or less.<ref name="hiawatha-story">{{Scribbins-Hiawatha-2007}}</ref>  While stricter regulations eventually reduced the speed limit to {{convert|79|mph}} along this stretch, plans are in place as of 2010 to improve the line to support speeds up to {{convert|110|mph}}—modestly faster than historic trains that used the route.<ref name="whitehouse">{{cite web|url=http://www.whitehouse.gov/the-press-office/fact-sheet-high-speed-intercity-passenger-rail-program-minneapolisst-paul-madison-m|title=Fact Sheet: High Speed Intercity Passenger Rail Program: Minneapolis/St. Paul - Madison - Milwaukee - Chicago|accessdate=June 6, 2010}}</ref>

